      "content": "This is a great question that gets to the heart of the \"Distillation\" problem in agentic workflows. Here is the breakdown of why both are likely necessary, but serve different masters:\n\n### 1. The \"Token Budget\" Argument\n**Lessons are the Linter; Experiences are the Tutorial.**\n*   **Lessons** are tiny. You can inject 20 lessons (constraints) for the price of 1 experience (procedural trace). \n*   If you only have experiences, you'll blow your context window trying to tell the agent every \"don't do X\" through a 15-line solve-path.\n*   **Utility**: Lessons are great for **negative constraints** (\"Don't use `os.Chdir` after `t.Parallel`\"). You don't need a procedural trace to learn that; you just need the agent to stop doing it.\n\n### 2. The \"Eligibility\" Argument\n**Lessons catch things before they become struggles.**\n*   **Experiences** are conditionally injected based on \"procedural need\" (i.e., when the agent is already stuck or starting a specific task).\n*   **Lessons** are always eligible. They act as a proactive safety net. If you only had experiences, the agent would have to *start* making a mistake before the system says, \"Wait, here's how we solved this last time.\"\n*   **Utility**: Lessons enforce **conventions** (\"All git tests must use `testutil.InitRepo`\").\n\n### 3. The \"Distillation\" Argument\n**Lessons are the \"Graduated\" form of Experiences.**\n*   If the agent hits the same git config error 5 times, it generates 5 experiences. \n*   The system eventually **derives** a single lesson: \"Use `config.NewEmpty` in tests.\" \n*   Once that lesson exists, the 5 experiences are redundant. The lesson is the \"compressed\" knowledge.\n*   **Utility**: Lessons represent **durable repository knowledge**; experiences represent **episodic memory**.\n\n### 4. When Experiences Win\n**Experiences are for \"Intuition.\"**\n*   If the task is \"Refactor the session strategy to be worktree-aware,\" a one-line lesson can't help you. \n*   You need the **Experience** to see the sequence of tools used, the files touched, and the \"avoid\" steps that saved the day last time.\n*   **Utility**: Experiences help with **navigation and trajectory**.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison at a Glance\n\n| Feature | **Lesson (Constraint)** | **Experience (Trajectory)** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Analogy** | A sign saying \"Bridge Out\" | A GPS trace of the detour |\n| **Best For** | Safety, Lints, Naming, Forbidden APIs | Refactoring, Debugging, Logic Flow |\n| **Cost** | Negligible (1-2 lines) | High (10-20 lines) |\n| **Injection** | Proactive (Always) | Reactive (When stuck) |\n\n**The Verdict**: If you only had Experiences, the agent would be smart but incredibly verbose and prone to repeating \"cheap\" mistakes until it looked up the relevant tutorial. If you only had Lessons, the agent would follow the rules but lack the \"gut feeling\" for how to solve complex, multi-step problems. \n\nThe value of **Lessons** is in their efficiency as a **\"No-Fly Zone\" map**, while **Experiences** are the **\"Flight Logs\"** that taught us where those zones are.",
